Certainly! Here's a simple example of a SwiftUI app that displays a list of items. This app will show a list of fruits, and when you tap on a fruit, it will display a detailed view with more information about that fruit.

```swift
import SwiftUI

// Define a model for the fruit
struct Fruit: Identifiable {
    var id = UUID()
    var name: String
    var description: String
}

// Define a view model to hold the list of fruits
class FruitViewModel: ObservableObject {
    @Published var fruits: [Fruit] = [
        Fruit(name: "Apple", description: "A sweet, edible fruit produced by an apple tree."),
        Fruit(name: "Banana", description: "A long, curved fruit with a yellow skin."),
        Fruit(name: "Cherry", description: "A small, round fruit with a red or black skin.")
    ]
}

// Define the main view
struct ContentView: View {
    @StateObject var viewModel = FruitViewModel()

    var body: some View {
        NavigationView {
            List(viewModel.fruits) { fruit in
                NavigationLink(destination: FruitDetailView(fruit: fruit)) {
                    Text(fruit.name)
                }
            }
            .navigationTitle("Fruits")
        }
    }
}

// Define the detail view
struct FruitDetailView: View {
    var fruit: Fruit

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            Text(fruit.name)
                .font(.largeTitle)
            Text(fruit.description)
                .font(.body)
        }
        .padding()
        .navigationTitle(fruit.name)
    }
}

// Define the App struct
@main
struct MyApp: App {
    var body: some Scene {
        WindowGroup {
            ContentView()
        }
    }
}
```

This SwiftUI app consists of three main components:

1. `Fruit`: A model representing a fruit with a name and description.
2. `FruitViewModel`: A view model that holds an array of fruits and notifies the view when the data changes.
3. `ContentView`: The main view that displays a list of fruits. When a fruit is tapped, it navigates to a detailed view of that fruit.
4. `FruitDetailView`: A view that displays the details of a selected fruit.
5. `MyApp`: The main app struct that sets up the window and displays the `ContentView`.

To run this app, you can create a new SwiftUI project in Xcode and replace the default content with the code above. Then, run the app on a simulator or a physical device to see the list of fruits and their details.

